---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const pageTitle =
  "Unit 5: Hash Functions and Hash Tables";
const unit = "Unit 5";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  unit={unit}
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">
    {pageTitle}
  </h1>
  <hr class="mb-6" />

  <!-- Hash Functions Overview -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Hash Functions Overview
    </summary>
    <p>
      A hash function converts a key
      into an index in a table. It
      should distribute keys uniformly
      and minimize collisions.
      Techniques include:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2">
      <li>
        <b>Division:</b> h(k) = k mod m; simple,
        may cluster.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Multiplication:</b> h(k) = floor(m
        * frac(k * A)); good distribution.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Folding:</b> Split key into parts,
        sum or combine.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Radix transformation:</b> Convert
        to another base, reduce modulo table
        size.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Digit rearrangement:</b> Reorder
        digits or bits.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Length-dependent:</b> Use key length
        with content.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Mid-square:</b> Square key, take
        middle digits; good for small keys.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Hash Function Analysis -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Estimating Effectiveness
    </summary>
    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2">
      <li>
        Uniformity: keys spread across
        table.
      </li>
      <li>Minimized collisions.</li>
      <li>Speed of computation.</li>
      <li>
        Independence: small changes in
        key affect hash.
      </li>
      <li>Resistance to patterns.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Hash Function Differentiation -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Differentiating Hash Functions
    </summary>
    <table
      class="table-auto border-collapse border border-gray-300 w-full text-sm"
    >
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Method</th
          >
          <th class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Strengths</th
          >
          <th class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Weaknesses</th
          >
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Division</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Simple, fast</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Clustering if m poorly
            chosen</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Multiplication</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Good distribution</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Requires fractional
            multiply</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Folding</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Works on long numeric keys</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Some bias possible</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Radix</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Handles different bases</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Requires conversion</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Digit rearrangement</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Obscures patterns</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Slow for large keys</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Length-dependent</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Uses key size info</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Weak if lengths similar</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Mid-square</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Good randomness for small
            numbers</td
          >
          <td class="border px-2 py-1"
            >Less effective for large
            keys</td
          >
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </details>

  <!-- Collision Resolution -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Collision Resolution Techniques
    </summary>
    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2">
      <li>
        <b>Open addressing:</b>
        <ul class="list-disc ml-6">
          <li>
            Linear probing: i → i+1
          </li>
          <li>
            Quadratic probing: i → i +
            c1·i² + c2·i²
          </li>
          <li>
            Double hashing: i → i +
            f(key)
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Separate chaining:</b>
        <ul class="list-disc ml-6">
          <li>
            Linked list at each slot
          </li>
          <li>List heads</li>
          <li>BST or dynamic arrays</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>Coalesced hashing</li>
      <li>Robin Hood hashing</li>
      <li>Cuckoo hashing</li>
      <li>Hopscotch hashing</li>
      <li>Dynamic resizing</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Hash Table Implementation -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Implementing Hash Tables
    </summary>
    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code class="language-c">
{`
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define TABLE_SIZE 10

typedef struct Node {
    int key;
    int value;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* table[TABLE_SIZE];

int hash(int key) {
    return key % TABLE_SIZE;
}

void insert(int key, int value) {
    int idx = hash(key);
    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->key = key;
    newNode->value = value;
    newNode->next = table[idx];
    table[idx] = newNode;
}

Node* search(int key) {
    int idx = hash(key);
    Node* curr = table[idx];
    while(curr) {
        if(curr->key == key) return curr;
        curr = curr->next;
    }
    return NULL;
}
`}
</code></pre>
  </details>

  <!-- Collision Detection -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Collision Detection and Analysis
    </summary>
    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2">
      <li>
        Check if slot is occupied on
        insert/search.
      </li>
      <li>
        Count collisions to measure hash
        quality.
      </li>
      <li>
        Analyze chain lengths or probe
        sequences.
      </li>
      <li>
        Load factor α = n / m; higher α
        → more collisions.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Hash Code Development -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Developing Hash Codes
    </summary>
    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2">
      <li>
        Select function type per key
        type.
      </li>
      <li>
        Ensure uniform distribution.
      </li>
      <li>
        Test with key patterns to reduce
        clustering.
      </li>
      <li>
        Combine techniques if needed
        (e.g., folding + division).
      </li>
      <li>
        Prefer prime table size for
        division method.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</BaseLayout>
